s the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a402785-5a82-51a9-a9a5-3630928c9333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427270 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)=\\frac{5}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427271 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427271 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427271 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427271 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-3sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427271 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3803507d-5804-5835-967d-6fb55640e457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427271 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky-3sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=-\\frac{15}{2}sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-\\frac{15}{2}sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{5}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427274 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427274 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427274 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427274 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427274 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd36548a-cee9-58ad-bcce-622c4e2dff50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427274 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =1.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435687 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435687 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435687 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0155fefb-60d8-5dc5-9cb9-bfd9b44bc962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435688 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435688 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435688 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04294cfb-cb8e-5e85-95b0-be9065b6019e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435689 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435689 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435689 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09521a1d-0037-59a4-b1e8-2b8b60a1d4de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435690 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435690 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb